{\plain {\b Exercise}{  Vary the frequencies
 }{\f1 w}{\fs16 \dn6 1}{ and }{\f1 w}{\fs16 
\dn6 2}{ and observe the quality of the 
FIR approximation.}}
}
.TXT 7 0 80 0
Cg a67.375000,68.000000,223
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
}
{\plain {\b Application }  Suppose that 
we wish to compute the second derivative
 of an analog waveform using the simple 
FIR digital filter we have been discussing.
 The analog signal is known, possibly through
 measurements, to be}
}
.EQN 9 5 83 0
{0:\n.1}NAME:2*{0:\p}NAME*(10)^(6)
.EQN 0 15 84 0
{0:\n.2}NAME:2*{0:\n.1}NAME
.EQN 0 16 82 0
{0:s.a}NAME({0:t}NAME):{0:cos}NAME({0:\n.1}NAME*{0:t}NAME)+2*{0:cos}NAME({0:\n.2}NAME*{0:t}NAME)
.TXT 5 -36 86 0
Cg a66.375000,68.000000,402
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
}
{\plain We must sample the analog waveform.
 We must choose the sampling frequency. 
We know that, according to Nyquist's Theorem,
 we must sample at twice the highest frequency
 of a real, lowpass waveform. But, further
 than that, here we wish to make sure that
 the highest frequency of the resulting 
digital signal is within the frequency range
 where our FIR well-approximates a second-order
 differentiator.}
}
.EQN 14 5 88 0
{0:\n.s}NAME:20*{0:\n.2}NAME
.EQN 0 14 89 0
{0:T.s}NAME:(2*{0:\p}NAME)/({0:\n.s}NAME)
.EQN 0 16 90 0
{0:T.s}NAME={0}?_n_u_l_l_
.TXT 6 -35 91 0
Cg a31.750000,68.000000,45
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
}
{\plain The sampled sequence, the digital
 signal, is}
}
.EQN 5 5 92 0
({0:s}NAME)[({0:k}NAME):{0:s.a}NAME({0:k}NAME*{0:T.s}NAME)
.TXT 5 -5 96 0
Cg a57.250000,68.000000,81
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
}
{\plain We see that the digital signal will
 be composed of two sinusoids, of frequencies}
}
.EQN 5 5 97 0
{0:\w.1}NAME:{0:\n.1}NAME*{0:T.s}NAME
.EQN 0 14 98 0
{0:\w.1}NAME={0}?_n_u_l_l_
.EQN 0 15 99 0
{0:\w.2}NAME:{0:\n.2}NAME*{0:T.s}NAME
.EQN 0 14 100 0
{0:\w.2}NAME={0}?_n_u_l_l_
.TXT 5 -48 101 0
Cg a67.250000,68.000000,93
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
}
{\plain These are within the band where 
the second-order derivative appears to be
 well approximated.}
}
.TXT 4 0 103 0
Cg a67.500000,68.000000,149
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
